Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.160
 

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.520
 

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.580
I'd like a coffee please

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.400
Oh, thanks.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.080
 

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.700
 

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.560
Hello everyone. And welcome to the Langfocus channel.
My name is Paul

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.300
Today, I'm going to talk about 
the languages of Switzerland.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.860
Switzerland is a fascinating country,
 known for many things such as:

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.420
the Swiss Alps, Swiss chocolate, 
Swiss watches, Swiss banking,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.220
but it's also known for being a multilingual nation
and a model of coexistence.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.320
Located at the crossroads of Germany, Austria, 
France, Italy and Liechtenstein,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.979
Switzerland has four recognized national languages.

00:00:52.979 --> 00:00:56.919
Those are German, French, Italian and Romansh.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:05.340
Three of those, German, French and Italian are official 
languages used by the state, but don't assume that 
everybody can speak four languages.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:12.340
Switzerland is a federation of 26 Cantons
which are like states with a high degree of autonomy.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.640
This federal type of government fits well 
with Switzerland's multilingualism.

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:22.299
At the national level Switzerland is essentially 
trilingual in German, French and Italian

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:30.160
That means that, for state purposes, you can use any 
of those three languages and those three languages
are used in Parliament and government offices.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.520
Romansh is recognized as a national language 
but it's not an official language of administration.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.900
Probably because there just aren't 
that many speakers of Romansh.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.660
The Swiss Constitution guarantees linguistic freedom 
for speakers of those four national languages,

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:52.080
meaning that they are free to use any of those 
languages to communicate with each other 
or with the government in their respective regions.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.280
Because Switzerland's linguistic communities 
are connected to specific territories,

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.680
the federal government gives each canton 
the right to choose its own official language

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:08.840
That effects which language is used at school, 
which language is used at government offices, 
on radio broadcasts and TV, all of those sorts of things.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:15.880
And in the cantons that have more than one official 
language, the canton grants each municipality,
the right to choose its own official language.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.759
That means that you sometimes have
one town with one official language
and the neighboring town with a different one.

00:02:21.759 --> 00:02:30.379
And there are even a handful of bilingual municipalities 
that provide services such as schools in both languages,
depending on where you live in that municipality.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.660
Now, let's take a look at each 
of the four national languages.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:42.420
The most widely spoken language is German, specifically Swiss German which is spoken 
by 5 million people or 63.5% of the population.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:49.800
And it's spoken in the largest area in northern, central 
and eastern Switzerland, as well as on the Swiss plateau
and part of the Swiss Alps.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:55.220
It is the official language of 17 cantons 
and it is co-official in 4 more cantons.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:03.240
Swiss German does not refer to one unified language 
but rather to a group of related alemannic dialects 
that are spoken inside Switzerland.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:11.120
These dialects are not considered inferior to standard
German and they are used for all aspects of daily life 
by basically all German speakers.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.700
Swiss German is generally unintelligible 
to speakers of standard German

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.560
but it isn't eligible to some Germans who live across 
the border in nearby areas who speak related dialects.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.659
And it's also understood by people in Liechtenstein 
as well as border areas of Austria.

00:03:25.759 --> 00:03:29.379
Aside from spoken Swiss German dialects, 
there is also standard Swiss German,

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:32.839
which is basically the same as 
the standard German spoken in Germany.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:38.819
In Switzerland, it is the written language and all Swiss 
German speakers learn the standard language in school.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:43.599
But basically in speech, they almost exclusively 
use Swiss German dialect.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.960
As I said before, speakers of standard German 
can't really understand Swiss German.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.340
And that includes Swiss people who learn German 
as a second language in school.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.900
That causes problems because, when they travel 
to the German speaking areas,

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:01.580
they find that the German speakers speak to them only
in Swiss German dialect and they can't understand it.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.260
Well, why doesn't the german-speaking population 
make more of an effort to speak standard German?

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.500
Well, according to some sources I've read, 
that was actually happening but then,
when World War 2 broke out,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.880
the Swiss German population wanted to be separate
from Germany, they wanted to be distinct,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.720
so they started de-emphasizing standard German 
and relying more on their spoken dialect.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:29.560
The second most widely spoken language is French
with 1.8 million native speakers or 22.5% of the population.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:35.080
The French-speaking region of Romandie is located 
in western Switzerland on the border with France.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.800
The French language in Switzerland differs very little 
from the French language in France.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.240
I've taken lessons with some teachers in Switzerland, 
I noticed very little difference.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:50.080
For example the number 70. 
In France, it's "soixante-dix" (sixty-ten)
but in Switzerland its "septante" (seventy)

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:55.760
Also, the number 90. 
In France, it's "quatre-vingt-dix" ("four-twenty-ten")
but in Switzerland is "nonante" ("ninety")

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.940
Or the word for "breakfast".
In France, it's "petit-déjeuner" but, in Switzerland, 
it's "déjeuner" which means "lunch" in France

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.780
And, in some areas, people use some German 
loanwords in their French but basically
the differences are very minor.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.900
There are also some loanwords from Franco-provençal

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:15.740
Franco-provençal is an unofficial regional language 
in France

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:23.320
and it is also still to some extent spoken in some 
of the rural areas in Switzerland but it has mostly 
become replaced by standard French.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.720
So, unlike the situation 
of Swiss German and standard German,

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.839
French speakers in Switzerland really have no problem
communicating with French speakers in France 
or Belgium or elsewhere.

00:05:32.839 --> 00:05:40.379
The third most widely spoken language is Italian 
with 645,000 native speakers or 8.1% of the population.

00:05:40.379 --> 00:05:43.859
It is an official language in only one canton called Ticino.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.160
And it is one of 3 official languages 
in one other canton called Graubunden.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.420
Both of those cantons are on the southern border with Italy.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:56.380
Swiss Italian is very similar to standard Italian 
and there really are no problems communicating

00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:01.440
between speakers of those two varieties
but there is a different kind of accent or intonation

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.379
and there has been some influence of Swiss German 
and French on the vocabulary, a little bit.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.720
And there's been some influence of 
the original Italian dialect of that area.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:19.380
Just like standard French has grown to replace 
Franco-provençal, standard Italian has grown to replace 
the original Italian dialect called Ticinese

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.439
It is actually quite different from standard Italian 
and it could be considered its own language.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:28.860
It is still spoken to some extent especially 
in rural areas and by elderly people

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.980
but it is basically being overtaken by Swiss Italian.

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:37.079
The least widely spoken of the four national languages, 
by a long shot, is Romansh.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:42.040
It has only 40,000 native speakers 
or 0.5% of the population.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:49.480
There are immigrant languages that have more speakers 
than Romansh. Serbo-croatian has almost three times 
as many speakers as Romansh.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:55.040
It is an official language in only one canton and
it's not the only one it's one of three official languages.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.700
It's that Canton Graubünden that I mentioned before.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.300
Romansh is a Romance language that developed 
from the spoken Latin of the Roman Empire.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:08.100
But it was also influenced by the local Celtic 
and Raetic languages of its region in Switzerland.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:13.860
It seems like a mystery why Romansh is even a national 
language at all because so few people speak it.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.379
But think about this: 
in 1939, Romansh was made an official language.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:24.020
That was right about the time when Hitler wanted 
to incorporate all ethnic Germans inside Germany

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:28.180
and Mussolini wanted to include 
all ethnic Italians inside Italy.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:32.320
So Switzerland might have felt nervous about that 
and decided to emphasize

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:40.160
that they are a different country, a multilingual, 
multi-ethnic country and they showed that 
by making Romansh an official language.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.600
According to some sources I've read, that was the case.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:48.340
About 10% of the population speak other languages, 
those people are mostly foreign workers or immigrants.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:52.600
The largest group is Serbo-croatians 
with about 100,000 speakers.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:57.700
Non-swiss people often assume that Swiss people 
are all quadrilingual, that they can speak 4 languages.

00:07:57.819 --> 00:07:59.219
But that's rarely the case.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:05.520
Language use is highly decentralized and it differs, 
depending on the region, the canton and even the town.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:12.840
Of course, Swiss people speak their own native language 
but then, from the 3rd grade of elementary school, 
they have to study a different national language.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:17.260
That's usually French for German speakers 
and German for everybody else.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:21.860
They also take English at school and, in high school, 
they have to study a third national language.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:27.540
Most people say that they are not fluent 
in a non-native national language but
that they can get by in one

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.960
But people aren't always very excited to speak 
a different national language.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:37.060
I hear that French speaking Swiss people 
don't really like to speak German and vice versa.

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:43.580
In the old days, they might have tried to speak 
the other language but, these days,
some people just use English instead.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:47.539
English is becoming more and more important 
in Switzerland because of globalization

00:08:47.539 --> 00:08:50.939
and because of the presence of 
more foreign companies in the big cities.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:56.040
Many Swiss people, these days, speak English better 
than they speak any of the other national languages.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.700
So some people want to emphasize English education 
in school instead of other national languages.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:06.080
And basically make English the lingua franca
between the different linguistic communities.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.080
But some people don't like that idea.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:13.520
They think that that undermines Switzerland's 
national identity and their multi-linguistic character.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:20.340
In the absence of a neutral lingua franca like English 
or some other language, communication can be a tricky issue.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.500
An issue that's different depending on the city and on the region.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:28.220
There are a handful of bilingual cities,
along the Röstigraben in Switzerland.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:34.480
The Röstigraben is basically the invisible linguistic 
boundary between French speaking and 
German-speaking Switzerland.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:42.160
In some of these bilingual cities, on one side of the river, 
you have French-speaking zones and, on the other side 
of the river, you have German-speaking zones.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:49.900
Cities like this are typically not integrated. 
The schools are not bilingual. They have separate schools 
with languages for the different communities.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.820
So, in a city like that, when you meet someone 
from the other side, which language do you speak?
It's always a tricky question.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:02.260
There are some multilingual cantons but sometimes,
they have disputes over linguistic policy or linguistic identity.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.740
And in one case, a whole new canton
was created to solve the dispute.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.940
The area of Jura was split off from the canton 
of Bern in 1977 and became its own canton.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:18.160
That is because Jura was primarily French-speaking 
while Bern was primarily German-speaking.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.960
But generally speaking the Swiss attitude towards 
multilingualism seems to be one of peaceful coexistence

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.840
with separate and distinct linguistic communities.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.080
That seems to be at the heart 
of what it means to be Swiss.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:35.480
The only problem is everyone wants to speak their own 
language and wait for the other side to bridge the gap.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.880
But, since all of the official languages are supposed to 
be equal and no preference is supposed to be shown,

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:47.120
then maybe a neutral lingua franca like English would be 
the best way to unite the different linguistic communities.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:51.740
These days some cantons want to stop teaching 
other national languages as second languages

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.120
and focus instead on English 
and make that the lingua franca.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:56.980
Do you think that is a good idea?

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.000
Let us know in the comments down below 
and we'll discuss it.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.860
Thank you for watching the Langfocus channel 
Have a good day.

